F66 for RISC OS
===============

F66 is MF66, a token-IR optimizing Forth: each definition is compiled to
AArch64 machine code as it is read, by JASM's own encoder (no LLVM), and
runs in the box's own process.  github.com/albanread/MF66.

Running
-------
  Double-click !F66      the console: a desktop window to try Forth in
  *F66                   the REPL at the command line; type bye to leave
  Double-click a Forth   (filetype &A77) runs it in a TaskWindow
  *F66 <file>            run a file (command line, TaskWindow, Obey)
  *F66 -i <file>         run a file, then the REPL
  *F66 -e "<forth>"      run one line
  *F66 -console [<file>] the console, including the file if one is given
  include <file>         load another file (Library.Declaritive, say)

The console
-----------
The output above, what you type and what the Forth prints, " ok" after
each line; the input line below it; the Words list at the side (newest
first) and the data stack in the status line, as .s shows it.
  Return                 run the line         Up, Down    history
  Left, Right, Home,     edit the line        Tab         complete a word
  Copy, Backspace        (Escape clears it)   F1          the word's stack
  Select on a word       put it in the line               comment
  Adjust on a word       its stack comment    ^ v         page the Words
  Drop a Forth file on the window or its icon bar icon to include it.
  Menu: Help on word, Clear (the output), Reset (a fresh Forth), Quit.
Escape stops a running word when it prints, calls a SWI, waits (ms) or
reads the keyboard; key and accept read from the console.  While the
console is open it has the Forth: *F66 (and double-clicking a Forth
file) says so until it is quit; drop the file on the console instead.

Errors in a file are reported as file:line: error: ...  A fault in Forth
code (a bad address, say) ends the run with an error; the box goes on.
Escape stops a program as it prints or calls a SWI.

SWIs
----
Forth addresses are RISC OS addresses: here, pad, create'd buffers and
strings can be given to a SWI as they are.

  regs              ( -- addr )   R0..R9, a cell each, then the flags
  sys               ( x0 .. xk-1 k n -- )  R0..Rk-1 from the stack, call n;
                                  an error is printed and THROWn
  xsys              ( x0 .. xk-1 k n -- err|0 )  the same, but the error
                                  (V set) is returned: its block, or 0
  swi / xswi        ( n -- ) / ( n -- err|0 )  with regs as they are
  r0 .. r9          ( -- x )      a register after the call
  swi-flags         ( -- addr )   N Z C V as bits 3..0
  swi" OS_Write0"   ( -- n )      a SWI's number, looked up when compiled
  s" name" swi#     ( -- n )      the same at run time (-1 if none)
  z" text"          ( -- c-addr ) a zero-terminated string
  zcount  .error    ( c-addr -- c-addr u ) and ( err -- ) print an error
  s>32 32>s         registers are 32 bits; they come back zero-extended

  : hello  z" Hello" 1 swi" OS_Write0" sys ;
  : ticks  0 swi" OS_ReadMonotonicTime" sys r0 ;

Files are RISC OS files (open-file and the rest); allocate gives RMA
memory; key, key? and accept read the keyboard.

Examples
--------
Graphics go to the screen: run Shapes, Bounce and Hanoi from the command
line (F12): *F66 <F66$Dir>.Examples.Shapes.  Calc, ToDo and Greeter are
desktop programs: *WimpTask F66 <F66$Dir>.Examples.Calc.

  Hello     printing and SWIs
  SWIs      by name and number, the X form, flags, a buffer
  Sieve     primes below 1 000 000, timed
  Machine   time and date, OS version, memory, variables, the screen mode
  Files     write, read back, OS_File's information, a directory listed
  Defining  CREATE and DOES>: arrays, counters, units, a table of actions
  RPN       a little calculator: an interpreter written in Forth
  Guess     a number guessing game: accept and key
  Shapes    OS_Plot: rectangles, discs, circles, a triangle, lines
  Bounce    an animation, with text placed by VDU 31 and coloured by VDU 17
  Hanoi     the Towers of Hanoi, solved by recursion and drawn as it goes
  Greeter   a desktop window from JSON, by the Declaritive module
            (include <F66$Dir>.Library.Declaritive)
  Calc      a calculator form: fields, buttons, an answer (Declaritive)
  ToDo      a to-do list: a list box, buttons and a clock (Declaritive)
